1
00:00:00,010 --> 00:00:02,390
Maya: New VC fund trying to break through.

2
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:04,700
Maya: That, and four more stories, on The Breakdown.

3
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:15,840
Alex: This is Bits and Tickers.

4
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,600
Alex: What got built, and what it's worth.

5
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:18,030
Alex: I'm Alex.

6
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:20,000
Maya: And I'm Maya.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,380
Maya: It's Thursday, November 12th, and this is The Breakdown: fewer stories,

8
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:26,730
Maya: with time to dig into each one.

9
00:00:26,730 --> 00:00:29,260
Maya: Everything we talk about is linked in the show notes.

10
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:31,360
Alex: On today's show.

11
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:34,000
Alex: New VC fund trying to break through.

12
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,920
Alex: AI revenue numbers just went ballistic.

13
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:39,320
Alex: AI characters you can actually talk to.

14
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:43,180
Alex: AI solves a really old quantum problem.

15
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:47,540
Alex: And Nvidia's AI financing is getting serious attention.

16
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:48,270
Alex: Let's get into it.

17
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:51,480
Maya: Time for The Ticker.

18
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:59,210
Maya: At Wednesday's close, the S&P 500 finished at 7,619, up 1.3 percent.

19
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:04,960
Maya: The Nasdaq Composite was up 1.6 percent and the Dow was up 0.5 percent.

20
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:09,120
Alex: And in big tech, Tesla had the best day, up 2.9 percent,

21
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:13,570
Alex: while Microsoft was at the other end, down 2.3 percent.

22
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:15,180
Alex: That's a snapshot, not advice.

23
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:20,360
Alex: TechCrunch has a piece on this guy, Olaf Sakkers,

24
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:22,180
Alex: who's trying to raise a second fund.

25
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:23,720
Maya: Right.

26
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:28,080
Maya: Apparently, it’s really hard to get in as a first-time fund manager these days.

27
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:29,860
Alex: Seriously.

28
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:35,200
Alex: PitchBook says established firms are grabbing almost everything – like 88% of the

29
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:36,680
Alex: VC dollars this year.

30
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:37,960
Maya: Since when?

31
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:39,560
Maya: That leaves a tiny sliver for newcomers.

32
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,220
Alex: It’s tough.

33
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:47,220
Alex: He started RedBlue Capital in 2021, closed a first fund with about five million.

34
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:50,400
Maya: Now he’s aiming for fifty million in the second.

35
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:51,100
Maya: A big jump.

36
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:55,280
Alex: Seems he’s been building trust, letting results speak for themselves,

37
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,900
Alex: which he says takes time.

38
00:01:57,510 --> 00:02:00,880
Maya: He writes smaller checks, fifty to one hundred thousand,

39
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:02,840
Maya: usually joining deals led by others.

40
00:02:03,310 --> 00:02:07,920
Alex: It’s interesting that he’s focusing on what he calls “the real economy,” like

41
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:09,500
Alex: robotics and manufacturing.

42
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:13,260
Maya: So, how does he actually get those LPs to commit,

43
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:16,340
Maya: especially when the established firms are hoovering up all the cash?

44
00:02:16,730 --> 00:02:18,860
Alex: Apparently, it’s a trust thing.

45
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:23,940
Alex: He says institutional investors are risk-averse, built to avoid zero returns,

46
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:25,840
Alex: so they stick with the known brands.

47
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:26,500
Maya: Go on.

48
00:02:26,906 --> 00:02:31,560
Alex: He had to prove it out with the first fund, backed by family offices,

49
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:35,100
Alex: then gradually bring in institutions for the second.

50
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:37,800
Alex: He’s deliberately letting the performance do the talking.

51
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:39,610
Maya: That’s a long game.

52
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:41,220
Maya: What was his background, anyway?

53
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:42,030
Maya: Anthropology?

54
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:47,500
Alex: Yeah, Princeton anthropology, a thesis on HIV in South Africa.

55
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:54,000
Alex: Then he was drafted into the Israeli military – desk job liaising with the U.S.

56
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,760
Alex: military, interestingly enough – and got into autonomous vehicles through that.

57
00:02:58,760 --> 00:02:59,750
Maya: Hold on, that's…

58
00:02:59,750 --> 00:03:00,120
Maya: a lot.

59
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:01,700
Alex: It is.

60
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,370
Alex: He was hired by Michael Granoff, who had capital to invest,

61
00:03:05,370 --> 00:03:10,380
Alex: and they built the first fund almost by accident, learning as they went.

62
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:13,760
Alex: Granoff had been an early backer of Better Place, by the way.

63
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:14,790
Maya: Huh.

64
00:03:14,790 --> 00:03:16,480
Maya: It sounds like he's leveraging connections.

65
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:21,640
Alex: I mean, it's a smart strategy, letting the results do the talking.

66
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:26,360
Alex: It’s a lot less stressful than chasing the big institutional commitments right away.

67
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:28,940
Maya: It sounds like a lot of luck to me, though.

68
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:33,360
Maya: Getting hired on the spot, building a fund "almost by accident"— that’s not

69
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:33,900
Maya: repeatable.

70
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:37,580
Alex: Maybe not, but he clearly has something – connections,

71
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:40,020
Alex: a knack for spotting opportunities.

72
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:42,760
Alex: Those five million dollars had to come from somewhere.

73
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:45,290
Maya: I think it's more about inertia.

74
00:03:45,290 --> 00:03:47,900
Maya: Established funds have this gravitational pull,

75
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,719
Maya: and it’s incredibly hard to break free from that.

76
00:03:51,070 --> 00:03:53,990
Alex: True, but he seems to understand the game.

77
00:03:53,990 --> 00:03:56,040
Alex: Small checks, letting others lead,

78
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:59,440
Alex: providing support— it's a way to get his foot in the door.

79
00:04:00,070 --> 00:04:02,440
Maya: It’s still a long shot, though, isn't it?

80
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:04,620
Maya: Getting to fifty million after starting with five?

81
00:04:05,070 --> 00:04:06,740
Alex: It's a test of endurance, he says.

82
00:04:07,310 --> 00:04:11,580
Maya: My read is that he's banking on the "gravity of perception" working in his favor

83
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:15,780
Maya: eventually, but I suspect that’s the most fragile part of his plan.

84
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:22,200
Maya: Apparently, OpenAI crossed $10 billion in quarterly revenue.

85
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:23,320
Maya: That’s according to TechCrunch.

86
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:24,220
Alex: Hold on, what?

87
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:28,230
Maya: Yeah, a nearly 50% jump from the second quarter.

88
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:30,680
Maya: It’s a significant increase in their growth rate, too.

89
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:33,420
Alex: Still, they weren't exactly struggling before.

90
00:04:34,044 --> 00:04:36,600
Maya: They were already growing at 18% previously.

91
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:44,560
Maya: Their adjusted operating margin improved slightly, too, from -150% to -109%.

92
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:45,800
Alex: That fast?

93
00:04:46,310 --> 00:04:50,720
Maya: They’re now talking about a $50 billion annual revenue run rate.

94
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:54,980
Maya: Anthropic, meanwhile, is claiming an $81 billion run rate.

95
00:04:55,470 --> 00:04:58,080
Alex: Their calculation methods are different, though, right?

96
00:04:58,340 --> 00:04:59,370
Maya: Exactly.

97
00:04:59,370 --> 00:05:01,560
Maya: They count sales through cloud partners,

98
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:03,780
Maya: while OpenAI doesn't for some of their sales.

99
00:05:04,390 --> 00:05:07,700
Alex: What’s driving this sudden acceleration, do you think?

100
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:09,920
Alex: It sounds like a lot to pull out of nowhere.

101
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:15,470
Maya: Apparently, they’ve been focused on tighter pricing, discounts, and better models.

102
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:19,780
Maya: They're also building more enterprise-focused products and these AI agents.

103
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:20,990
Alex: Really?

104
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:23,640
Alex: I thought they were lagging behind Anthropic in that space.

105
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:28,970
Maya: They were, but they’ve been trying to catch up, shifting focus to those areas.

106
00:05:28,970 --> 00:05:30,900
Maya: It’s interesting that they’re pulling ahead now.

107
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:35,960
Alex: Just a slight improvement in operating margin though - still a massive loss.

108
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:42,520
Maya: It narrowed from a 150% negative margin to 109%, yes.

109
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:45,580
Maya: It's a small shift, but in that scale it’s still significant.

110
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:48,560
Alex: $81 billion for Anthropic?

111
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:48,780
Alex: Wow.

112
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:52,400
Maya: That’s their annual revenue run rate, per their claim,

113
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,640
Maya: and it’s due to those sales through cloud partners.

114
00:05:55,250 --> 00:05:59,300
Maya: I think it’s a bit misleading to get excited about this run rate stuff, honestly.

115
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:01,240
Maya: It’s just a calculation.

116
00:06:01,910 --> 00:06:02,420
Alex: Hold on.

117
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:03,600
Alex: Fifty billion?

118
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:04,740
Alex: That’s still a lot of revenue.

119
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:08,910
Maya: It is, but it’s still burning a ton of cash, Alex.

120
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:11,840
Maya: That operating margin narrowing isn't exactly a victory.

121
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:17,480
Alex: True, but they are shifting focus to enterprise, and that seems to be paying off,

122
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:18,620
Alex: at least according to this.

123
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:20,550
Maya: I’m less convinced.

124
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:23,220
Maya: It sounds like they’re just trying to catch up to Anthropic now.

125
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:27,560
Alex: I'm not so sure they're "catching up." They're just taking a different route.

126
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:32,480
Maya: Maybe, but I still think the hype around these run rates is obscuring the fact that

127
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,740
Maya: they're still losing money hand over fist.

128
00:06:35,660 --> 00:06:40,160
Alex: Ultimately, it just shows how much demand there is for what they're building,

129
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:42,860
Alex: even if the current business model isn't sustainable.

130
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:49,620
Alex: So, Adobe’s been showing off some experimental stuff at their Max conference – did

131
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:51,580
Alex: you see anything that caught your eye?

132
00:06:51,910 --> 00:06:54,260
Maya: Project Screen Test, mostly.

133
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:59,540
Maya: Apparently, you can now have conversations with AI-generated characters to, like,

134
00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:00,700
Maya: shape their personalities.

135
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:02,420
Alex: Since when is that a thing?

136
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:02,940
Alex: Sounds...

137
00:07:02,940 --> 00:07:03,700
Alex: involved.

138
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:07,210
Maya: It's designed to give creators more control.

139
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:09,830
Maya: You start by giving them personality traits,

140
00:07:09,830 --> 00:07:13,240
Maya: then chat with them to flesh out their backstory, goals, all that.

141
00:07:13,770 --> 00:07:17,020
Alex: And it generates animated tests based on those conversations?

142
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:18,060
Maya: It does.

143
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:23,200
Maya: My read is that it’s all about addressing the lack of creative control in AI video

144
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:23,880
Maya: generation.

145
00:07:24,420 --> 00:07:25,760
Alex: Sounds like a bunch of demos.

146
00:07:26,390 --> 00:07:27,200
Maya: Still, it feels…

147
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:29,090
Maya: clunky, doesn't it?

148
00:07:29,090 --> 00:07:32,200
Maya: I mean, what’s the actual process for giving the character a voice?

149
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:35,800
Alex: You can upload sample audio, apparently.

150
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:38,940
Alex: Or describe it in a prompt - they’ll try to match it.

151
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:41,040
Alex: It’s not like they’re generating from scratch.

152
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,540
Maya: So you're basically building a digital puppet?

153
00:07:43,970 --> 00:07:44,670
Alex: Kind of.

154
00:07:44,670 --> 00:07:50,660
Alex: The article mentioned it’s because existing AI video tools lack creative control;

155
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:54,440
Alex: traditional animation has absolute intention at every step.

156
00:07:54,980 --> 00:07:55,880
Maya: Good to know.

157
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:57,000
Maya: Sounds like a neat trick, though.

158
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:58,920
Alex: Definitely a demo.

159
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:01,040
Alex: I'm not seeing how that becomes a workflow.

160
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:05,580
Alex: It's a clever way to inject some personality, I suppose,

161
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:09,520
Alex: but it still feels like a lot of steps for something that could be done more simply.

162
00:08:10,350 --> 00:08:14,500
Maya: It's not about being simple; it's about giving creators a tool that wasn't there

163
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:14,880
Maya: before.

164
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:17,100
Alex: But is it a useful tool?

165
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:18,140
Alex: It sounds like a hobby.

166
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:23,020
Maya: I think it addresses a real pain point in the video generation space.

167
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:25,000
Maya: The article explicitly said so.

168
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:25,600
Alex: Maybe.

169
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:27,750
Alex: Still, building a digital puppet…

170
00:08:27,750 --> 00:08:28,680
Alex: it’s a weird framing.

171
00:08:29,290 --> 00:08:30,960
Maya: It’s a metaphor, Alex.

172
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:32,640
Maya: It's illustrating the level of control.

173
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:37,890
Alex: A metaphor that doesn't really translate to a practical workflow, in my view.

174
00:08:38,410 --> 00:08:41,580
Maya: It's a first iteration, and even in that state,

175
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:44,930
Maya: it's demonstrably an improvement over what’s available now,

176
00:08:44,930 --> 00:08:46,516
Maya: so it’s already valuable to some.

177
00:08:48,020 --> 00:08:53,560
Maya: According to TechCrunch, a startup called PSI used an Anthropic model to crack an

178
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:56,000
Maya: 18-year-old problem in quantum computing.

179
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:56,640
Alex: Hang on.

180
00:08:56,640 --> 00:08:56,940
Alex: Seriously?

181
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:02,510
Maya: Yeah, a recent MIT grad, David Turturean, apparently did it.

182
00:09:02,510 --> 00:09:04,920
Maya: He’s deferred his master’s to work at PSI.

183
00:09:04,937 --> 00:09:05,437
Alex: Wow.

184
00:09:05,730 --> 00:09:10,570
Maya: The problem has to do with how quickly quantum computers can solve certain issues,

185
00:09:10,570 --> 00:09:14,890
Maya: and Scott Aaronson, one of the original researchers, says he’s…

186
00:09:14,890 --> 00:09:16,900
Maya: well, he's got a lot of feelings about it.

187
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:17,500
Alex: Is that all?

188
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:22,300
Maya: It’s a negative result, apparently closing one door on quantum computing,

189
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:27,400
Maya: and PSI’s CEO, Matt Pines, is saying theoretical physics is going to be mostly

190
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:28,560
Maya: machines’ work soon.

191
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:29,240
Alex: Bold claim.

192
00:09:29,763 --> 00:09:34,420
Maya: Their current revenue comes from optimizing data centers – simulating things like

193
00:09:34,420 --> 00:09:39,180
Maya: heat and power – but the real goal is to turn these breakthroughs into actual tech.

194
00:09:39,610 --> 00:09:41,040
Alex: How did he even do it?

195
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:41,260
Alex: Just…

196
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:46,680
Alex: a recent grad and an Anthropic model solving something that’s stumped people for 18

197
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:47,120
Alex: years?

198
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:50,290
Maya: Well, it wasn't just any Anthropic model.

199
00:09:50,290 --> 00:09:53,240
Maya: It was Fable 5.2, an unreleased one.

200
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:57,960
Maya: And he used what they’re calling specialized model harnesses and scaffolding,

201
00:09:57,960 --> 00:09:59,200
Maya: which apparently is key.

202
00:09:59,340 --> 00:09:59,680
Alex: Harnesses?

203
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:00,440
Alex: What are those?

204
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:01,340
Maya: It’s like…

205
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:06,410
Maya: tools and memory systems, helping the model really dig into the problem.

206
00:10:06,410 --> 00:10:10,680
Maya: The CEO, Pines, says they need a lot of that to tackle the work properly.

207
00:10:11,270 --> 00:10:12,670
Alex: Feels like a demo, doesn’t it?

208
00:10:12,940 --> 00:10:17,620
Maya: Aaronson, one of the original researchers, seemed to think so too, initially.

209
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:22,380
Maya: He was surprised it hadn’t shown up in OpenAI’s Mathocalypse,

210
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:24,020
Maya: wondering if it was so much harder.

211
00:10:24,580 --> 00:10:26,600
Alex: So, is PSI just doing this for fun?

212
00:10:26,990 --> 00:10:28,370
Maya: Not exactly.

213
00:10:28,370 --> 00:10:31,450
Maya: They’re currently making money optimizing data centers,

214
00:10:31,450 --> 00:10:35,820
Maya: but Pines is pitching the longer-term vision: turning these theoretical physics

215
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:38,530
Maya: breakthroughs into actual technology.

216
00:10:38,530 --> 00:10:42,740
Maya: He says he had to stretch to convince investors six to nine months ago that this

217
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:43,420
Maya: was possible.

218
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:47,160
Maya: I think Pines is just playing the investor game, really.

219
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:51,120
Maya: Saying theoretical physics will be mostly machines’ work soon?

220
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:52,700
Maya: That’s a sales pitch.

221
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:53,360
Alex: Hmm.

222
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:55,740
Alex: It’s impressive, though, isn’t it?

223
00:10:55,740 --> 00:10:59,220
Alex: A recent grad solving something that’s been open for so long.

224
00:10:59,780 --> 00:11:00,720
Maya: Sure, impressive.

225
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:02,270
Maya: But it's a demo.

226
00:11:02,270 --> 00:11:04,070
Maya: Aaronson himself seemed to think that.

227
00:11:04,410 --> 00:11:06,640
Alex: But the harnesses and scaffolding thing…

228
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:10,950
Alex: that does suggest something more than just throwing a model at a problem.

229
00:11:11,490 --> 00:11:15,080
Maya: Maybe, but ultimately, it’s still just one result.

230
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:19,720
Maya: They're chasing a long game, and data center optimization is the real money-maker

231
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:20,460
Maya: right now.

232
00:11:20,860 --> 00:11:25,420
Alex: That's true, but the fact that they were able to crack something so fundamental,

233
00:11:25,420 --> 00:11:27,890
Alex: even with a specialized setup…

234
00:11:27,890 --> 00:11:29,530
Alex: it indicates a potential shift.

235
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:33,720
Maya: I’m not convinced it’s a shift; it's a carefully managed narrative.

236
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:37,320
Alex: Still, someone built something that could do this.

237
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:39,820
Alex: And that’s a worthwhile accomplishment in itself.

238
00:11:41,550 --> 00:11:47,840
Alex: This CNBC piece about Nvidia being, essentially, the central bank of AI,

239
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:52,200
Alex: caught my eye because it’s a fascinating way to think about how interconnected

240
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:54,360
Alex: everything has become.

241
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:59,500
Alex: Apparently, they’ve made huge financial commitments to other firms to help grow the

242
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:00,060
Alex: industry.

243
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:01,780
Maya: Hundreds of billions?

244
00:12:01,780 --> 00:12:02,290
Maya: That’s…

245
00:12:02,740 --> 00:12:07,100
Alex: Yeah, and it’s got the Federal Reserve and other financial institutions watching

246
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:11,240
Alex: closely, just in case something unexpected happens.

247
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:15,440
Alex: They’re worried about a "broad-based repricing of assets," apparently.

248
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:20,360
Maya: Hold on, so is someone saying we’re headed for another 2008 situation?

249
00:12:20,730 --> 00:12:23,000
Alex: Sheila Bair, a former regulator,

250
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:27,470
Alex: says Nvidia isn’t "too big to fail" in the same way a bank is.

251
00:12:27,470 --> 00:12:30,840
Alex: They still make a product people want, and have a big equity buffer.

252
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:32,520
Maya: Makes sense.

253
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:36,640
Maya: But she does say regulators should still watch for exposure across the financial

254
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:37,080
Maya: system.

255
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:41,040
Alex: And then there's the whole FBI and Congress thing,

256
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:44,960
Alex: reportedly concerning Huang's relationship with someone.

257
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:47,320
Alex: The board still has confidence in him, though.

258
00:12:47,820 --> 00:12:49,980
Maya: It’s a lot to juggle, even for Nvidia.

259
00:12:50,630 --> 00:12:54,240
Maya: So, how exactly does this "central bank of AI" thing work?

260
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:54,520
Maya: Just…

261
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:55,680
Maya: handing out money?

262
00:12:56,060 --> 00:12:57,130
Alex: Pretty much.

263
00:12:57,130 --> 00:13:02,110
Alex: They’re providing financial commitments, essentially loans or guarantees,

264
00:13:02,110 --> 00:13:06,440
Alex: to other companies to help them build out their AI infrastructure.

265
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:10,110
Alex: It's not just cash, it's a whole web of financial support.

266
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:14,160
Maya: Hold on, what happens if those companies can’t pay back those commitments?

267
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:15,900
Alex: That’s the worry.

268
00:13:15,900 --> 00:13:19,100
Alex: If Nvidia’s GPU chip values drop,

269
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:23,120
Alex: it could trigger a chain reaction where companies struggle to repay what they

270
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:24,220
Alex: borrowed to buy them.

271
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:24,820
Maya: That’s…

272
00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:25,260
Maya: concerning.

273
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:30,460
Alex: Sheila Bair pointed out that even though Nvidia isn't structurally the same as a

274
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:35,840
Alex: bank, that ripple effect is what regulators are paying attention to – the exposure

275
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:37,340
Alex: across the wider system.

276
00:13:37,850 --> 00:13:41,100
Maya: And the Huang situation complicates things, doesn’t it?

277
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:41,960
Maya: It’s a weird timing.

278
00:13:42,590 --> 00:13:44,160
Alex: It certainly does.

279
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:48,800
Alex: The article mentions analysts are now considering a range of outcomes,

280
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:51,440
Alex: including his potential removal, which would be…

281
00:13:51,440 --> 00:13:52,580
Alex: quite a shift.

282
00:13:53,210 --> 00:13:58,930
Alex: I think the “central bank of AI” analogy is really underselling the risk here.

283
00:13:58,930 --> 00:14:01,800
Alex: It’s not about Nvidia failing outright,

284
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:06,220
Alex: it’s about those financial commitments suddenly becoming a problem for a lot of

285
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:06,900
Alex: other companies.

286
00:14:07,620 --> 00:14:10,340
Maya: I see it more as a symptom than a cause.

287
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:13,880
Maya: Nvidia is just capitalizing on the existing demand,

288
00:14:13,880 --> 00:14:17,520
Maya: providing the financing that already exists somewhere else in the system.

289
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:22,260
Alex: Still, a lot of money is tied up in their continued success.

290
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:26,480
Alex: Huang’s situation adds a layer of unpredictable volatility, doesn't it?

291
00:14:26,690 --> 00:14:28,570
Maya: It's certainly distracting.

292
00:14:28,570 --> 00:14:31,780
Maya: But the board's confidence in him is important to note,

293
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:33,580
Maya: it’s not like they’re in crisis mode.

294
00:14:33,960 --> 00:14:38,420
Alex: True, but that doesn't negate the potential for a broad-based repricing.

295
00:14:38,420 --> 00:14:39,660
Alex: It’s a delicate balance.

296
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:44,650
Maya: My read is that the real concern isn’t Nvidia itself collapsing,

297
00:14:44,650 --> 00:14:49,920
Maya: but the interconnectedness of the whole AI buildout finally revealing its fragility.

298
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:50,520
Alex: Makes sense.

299
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:53,260
Maya: The worry isn't that Nvidia will fail,

300
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:58,540
Maya: but that its success has masked a systemic reliance on a single point of potential

301
00:14:58,540 --> 00:14:59,040
Maya: failure.

302
00:15:00,620 --> 00:15:01,700
Alex: That's the news.

303
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:02,560
Alex: Time for The Takeaway.

304
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:07,480
Maya: I think the common thread here is just how much money is sloshing around,

305
00:15:07,480 --> 00:15:09,760
Maya: enabling all this experimentation, right?

306
00:15:10,140 --> 00:15:15,340
Alex: I'll be watching to see how quickly those Adobe AI features actually ship – demos

307
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:15,900
Alex: are easy.

308
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:22,320
Maya: I'm curious to see if PSI can actually translate their AI discovery efforts into

309
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:23,870
Maya: something beyond a research grant.

310
00:15:24,330 --> 00:15:29,260
Alex: Hold on, I'm not writing off Olaf Sakkers' approach just because it's niche.

311
00:15:29,260 --> 00:15:31,320
Alex: Someone’s gotta find the inefficiencies.

312
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:36,530
Maya: I still maintain Nvidia’s financing web isn’t that surprising, Alex.

313
00:15:36,530 --> 00:15:37,080
Maya: It's just…

314
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:37,940
Maya: infrastructure.

315
00:15:38,790 --> 00:15:43,640
Alex: I guess we’ll agree to disagree on how much that signifies about systemic risk.

316
00:15:45,260 --> 00:15:46,860
Maya: And that's The Breakdown.

317
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:49,540
Maya: Every story we talked about is linked in the show notes.

318
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:52,260
Alex: Next up is The Long Drive, tomorrow.

319
00:15:52,260 --> 00:15:55,700
Alex: One reminder: nothing here is financial advice.

320
00:15:55,700 --> 00:15:58,080
Alex: From Bits and Tickers, I'm Alex.

321
00:15:58,080 --> 00:15:59,140
Alex: Keep building.

322
00:15:59,156 --> 00:15:59,680
Maya: I'm Maya.

323
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:00,720
Maya: And keep counting.
